{
  "dom_id": "step-45333",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14251512/assets/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-45333\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block\" stepid=\"45333\" steptype=\"tutorial\" contentid=\"7860\">\n            <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                <div class=\"stepName\">\n                    <a id=\"stepAnchor-45333\" class=\"stepAnchor\" name=\"step-1\">Introduction</a>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"45333\">?</div>\n            </div> \n\n            <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n                            transition: none;\n                            -webkit-transition: none;\n                            -moz-transition: none;\n                            -ms-transition: none;\n                            -o-transition: none\n                            }\n\n                            .mjx-svg-href {\n                            fill: blue;\n                            stroke: blue\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox {\n                            display: table!important\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox span {\n                            display: table-cell!important;\n                            width: 10000em!important;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage__block {\n                            text-align: center;\n                            margin: 1em 0em;\n                            position: relative;\n                            display: block!important;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            width: 100%\n                            }</style><p>The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.</p>\n<table rules=\"all\" cellpadding=\"10\" align=\"center\" style=\"border:1px solid black; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 4mm; margin-top: 4mm\"> \n<tbody><tr> \n<th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" style=\"text-align:center\">Surface</th>\n<th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" style=\"text-align:center\">Equation</th>\n<th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" style=\"text-align:center\">Image</th>\n</tr>\n<!-- Ellipsoid -->\n<tr> \n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Ellipsoid</t